  {
    "episode_id": 72740,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72740.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 39,
    "turn_text": "We should be able to work together. When I first came to Congress, we did. It's a good, kind of started this polarization and it's got worse and worse and worse. But our leadership in the discussions that I've had with them and what they've said in front of the caucus is that they intend to focus on the issues that affect Americans, health care, infrastructure, you know, the issues that people have brought to us during the election. You know, and they want to work with the president. They want to work with the Senate. And so I don't see why we couldn't work together, but you know, it's you got too many people that are in districts where they have no competition from the other party. And that's a lot of the problem. And so their challenge, electorally, is is not defeating the other party that it's defeating somebody in their own party which pushes people to the left and right and that that's the biggest problem and it's not really a rural urban thing so much is a party thing and until we get some more balance in the redistricting i don't know how we get around that but um... people gotta want to be able to work together i mean if you could easily You know, I could have easily blown up this farm bill, you know, and I worked very hard to help the chairman become successful and get this thing done. And I had to give up some things I wanted to do to make it happen. And that's what you have to do to make this work.",
